Drew,

Nick didn't exactly supply the answer's in an easy to understand form, nor
did he include the actual coordinates of cities, so I've processed them
a little.

The 30 cities (i.e. coordinate pairs, one to a line) should be in the
order that gives the optimal path.

At our next meeting, I can explain how Nick's algorithm works.  It doesn't
make your stuff irrelevant, as the run time of Nick's algorithm grows
exponentially with problem size, and so in practise his algorithm can't solve
problems much larger than 30 cities.  The number after "Number of 
partial expanded is" indicates how many different paths his algorithm
has examined while finding the optimal ... you'll see some big numbers.


